1. Introduction}
{\hich\af4\afs22\alang1033\dbch\afs22\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\par}\pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\kerning1\rtlch\af8\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af10\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\f4\fs22\lang1033

{\hich\af4\afs22\alang1033\dbch\afs22\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\par}\pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\kerning1\rtlch\af8\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af10\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa200\ltrpar{\f4\fs22\lang1033
Entropy is usually treated as a static property measured through distributions, randomness tests, or statistical uniformity. But entropy is not static. It unfolds. It moves. It evolves over time. And when entropy is examined as a temporal process rather than a purely statistical one, a deeper structure emerges: patterns of drift, curvature, oscillation, basin formation, and attractor behavior that remain invisible to conventional randomness analysis. This work introduces and formalizes temporal geometry, the study of how entropy behaves as a dynamical system.}
{\hich\af4\afs22\alang1033\dbch\afs22\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\par}\pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\kerning1\rtlch\af8\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af10\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\f4\fs22\lang1033

{\hich\af4\afs22\alang1033\dbch\afs22\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\par}\pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\kerning1\rtlch\af8\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af10\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa200\ltrpar{\f4\fs22\lang1033
The foundation of temporal geometry is the observation that entropy sources produce distinct, reproducible temporal fingerprints when mapped into symbolic or numeric manifolds. Early experiments using the hexagram manifold revealed measurable curvature, basin depth, and drift hierarchy across hardware, atmospheric, symbolic, and inverted symbolic entropy sources. These findings suggested that symbolic manifolds act as temporal filters or structures that reveal the underlying temporal behavior of entropy rather than its statistical randomness.}
{\hich\af4\afs22\alang1033\dbch\afs22\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\par}\pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\kerning1\rtlch\af8\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af10\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\f4\fs22\lang1033

{\hich\af4\afs22\alang1033\dbch\afs22\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\par}\pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\kerning1\rtlch\af8\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af10\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa200\ltrpar{\f4\fs22\lang1033
Over the course of extended experimentation, the scope of this work expanded dramatically. New manifolds were introduced: byte, 16}{\f6\fs22\lang1033
\_}{\f4\fs22\lang1033
bit, 64}{\f6\fs22\lang1033
\_}{\f4\fs22\lang1033
bit, and raw temporal dynamics without clustering. New entropy sources were added: multiple hardware devices, atmospheric RNG, tunneling entropy, symbolic encodings, inverted encodings, PRNG drift, and high}{\f6\fs22\lang1033
\_}{\f4\fs22\lang1033
resolution Android entropy probes. Across more than a dozen datasets, one phenomenon appeared universally: temporal oscillation. Regardless of device, encoding, resolution, or manifold, entropy exhibited stable, repeating patterns of temporal drift.}
{\hich\af4\afs22\alang1033\dbch\afs22\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\par}\pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\kerning1\rtlch\af8\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af10\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\f4\fs22\lang1033

{\hich\af4\afs22\alang1033\dbch\afs22\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\par}\pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\kerning1\rtlch\af8\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af10\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa200\ltrpar{\f4\fs22\lang1033
While traditional time-series analysis applies tools like Takens' delay-coordinate embedding or Recurrence Quantification Analysis (RQA) to detect determinism in chaotic systems, these methods are typically deployed to study single, continuous physical or biological attractors rather than comparative entropy evolution. Conversely, classical information theory evaluates entropy through static probability distributions (Shannon) or algorithmic complexity (Kolmogorov), treating temporal fluctuations as noise to be mitigated. This work bridges that methodological divide by introducing a multi-manifold temporal geometry framework. By examining how diverse stochastic streams ranging from quantum-adjacent LED electron tunneling to hardware PRNG drift project into structured symbolic and numeric manifolds, we demonstrate that temporal dynamics exhibit universal, resolution-invariant invariants that transcend individual source statistics.}
{\hich\af4\afs22\alang1033\dbch\afs22\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\par}\pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\kerning1\rtlch\af8\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af10\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\f4\fs22\lang1033

{\hich\af4\afs22\alang1033\dbch\afs22\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\par}\pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\kerning1\rtlch\af8\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af10\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa200\ltrpar{\f4\fs22\lang1033
This paper consolidates the full body of temporal}{\f6\fs22\lang1033
\_}{\f4\fs22\lang1033
geometry research into a single scientific report. It presents the computational pipeline used to analyze entropy sources across multiple manifolds and resolutions; demonstrates resolution invariance and manifold independence; establishes curvature, drift, and basin metrics as reliable temporal descriptors; and provides empirical evidence that temporal geometry is a real, measurable, reproducible domain.}
{\hich\af4\afs22\alang1033\dbch\afs22\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\par}\pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\kerning1\rtlch\af8\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af10\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\f4\fs22\lang1033

{\hich\af4\afs22\alang1033\dbch\afs22\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\par}\pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\kerning1\rtlch\af8\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af10\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa200\ltrpar{\f4\fs22\lang1033
The central thesis of this work is that entropy contains temporal structure, and that symbolic and numeric manifolds reveal that structure. Temporal geometry enables classification of entropy sources by their temporal behavior rather than their statistical randomness. The discovery of universal oscillation across all datasets suggests that temporal geometry is not an artifact of encoding or measurement but rather a fundamental property of entropy evolution.}
{\hich\af4\afs22\alang1033\dbch\afs22\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\par}\pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\kerning1\rtlch\af8\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af10\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\f4\fs22\lang1033

{\hich\af4\afs22\alang1033\dbch\afs22\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\par}\pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\kerning1\rtlch\af8\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af10\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa200\ltrpar{\f4\fs22\lang1033
2. Materials and Methods}
{\hich\af4\afs22\alang1033\dbch\afs22\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\par}\pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\kerning1\rtlch\af8\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af10\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\f4\fs22\lang1033

{\hich\af4\afs22\alang1033\dbch\afs22\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\par}\pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\kerning1\rtlch\af8\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af10\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa200\ltrpar{\f4\fs22\lang1033
The methodological framework for this study is designed to measure temporal behavior in entropy without depending on encoding, resolution, device, or manifold. To accomplish this, a resolution invariant and manifold independent computational pipeline was developed and applied to a wide range of entropy sources. These sources include hardware entropy from multiple machines, atmospheric entropy from Random.org, tunneling entropy, symbolic encodings, inverted encodings, PRNG drift captured through the Temporal Thermometer\u8239\'3f2.0 Android probe, and raw temporal dynamics that were analyzed without clustering. Each dataset represents a distinct modality and was processed independently before being compared across manifolds to evaluate temporal geometry invariance.}
{\hich\af4\afs22\alang1033\dbch\afs22\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\par}\pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\kerning1\rtlch\af8\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af10\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\f4\fs22\lang1033

{\hich\af4\afs22\alang1033\dbch\afs22\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\par}\pard\plain \s0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\kerning1\rtlch\af8\afs24\alang1081\ltrch\hich\af3\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af10\langfe2052\loch\f3\fs24\lang1033\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa200\ltrpar{\f4\fs22\lang1033
Tunneling entropy was collected using a custom LED electron tunneling noise generator designed specifically for this study. The circuit consists of a dual LM358P operational amplifier powered by a 5\u8239\'3fV USB input. A 4.7\u8239\'3fk}{\f4\fs22\lang1032
\u937\'3f bias resistor and a 0.1}{\f4\fs22\lang1033
\u8239\'3f\u181\'b5F coupling capacitor form the tunneling junction across a clear epoxy LED, producing stochastic electron tunneling events that generate a broadband noise signal. This signal is amplified through the LM358P and coupled via a 0.1\u8239\'3f\u181\'b5F output capacitor to a 3.5\u8239\'3fmm stereo audio interface connected to a Sabrent USB sound card. The schematic and physical layout are shown in Figure\u8239\'3f1. The probe was mounted on a foam block with female}{\f6\fs22\lang1033
\_}{\f4\fs22\lang1033
female jumper wires and DIP}{\f6\fs22\lang1033
\_}{\f4\fs22\lang1033
8 breadboard adapters to ensure mechanical stability and electrical isolation. The resulting analog signal was digitized at 48\u8239\'3fkHz and recorded as raw entropy data for subsequent temporal}{\f6\fs22\lang1033
\_}{\f4\fs22\lang1033
geometry analysis. This device served as the primary hardware entropy source for the tunneling datasets used in this study.}
